<figure class="wp-block-image alignwide size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="697" src="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Yawp_Figure_3.1-1-2-1024x697.jpg" alt="The first modern atlas, called the Theatrum Orbis Terrarum by Dutch Cartographer Abraham Ortellus, 1572. The continents are labeled in Latin, with distorted and inaccurate shapes reflecting early geographic knowledge. Africa is prominently centered and colored yellow, Europe and Asia are pink, and the Americas are orange. The oceans are labeled, and artistic clouds and sea monsters decorate the map's border. " class="wp-image-1906" srcset="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Yawp_Figure_3.1-1-2-1024x697.jpg 1024w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Yawp_Figure_3.1-1-2-300x204.jpg 300w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Yawp_Figure_3.1-1-2-768x523.jpg 768w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Yawp_Figure_3.1-1-2-1536x1046.jpg 1536w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Yawp_Figure_3.1-1-2-2048x1394.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Figure 3.1. The first modern atlas, Theatrum Orbis Terrarum, by Abraham Ortelius, World, 1572. Wikimedia.</figcaption></figure>







